BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Birds and Our Christmas Traditions
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261208T150000
UID:6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Birds are woven into the fabric of Christmas traditions\, serv
 ing as powerful symbols of hope\, renewal\, and warmth during the winter m
 onths. Their presence during the holidays\, whether through festive orname
 nts\, songs\, or sightings at feeders\, recalls ancient lore that connects
  birds with messages of love and positivity. By inviting birds into our li
 ves and honoring them through tradition\, we not only support these treasu
 red visitors but also embrace the spirit of Christmas—one of generosity\
 , harmony\, and the celebration of nature’s enduring beauty. Jim Mackie
 wicz is the owner of Wild Birds Unlimited and has been helping customers w
 ith backyard bird feeding and connecting to nature for 25 years.\nInformat
 ion Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected
  by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 2
 0(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and 
 program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the li
 brary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nans
 werline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Pleasant\, Basement program room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3963a8dcc1022f003a66e2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
